      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pat the sardine fillets dry. Dredge in plain flour and shake off excess. Heat 4 tablespoons of olive oil in a frying pan over medium-high heat. Fry the sardines in batches for 2 minutes each side until golden. Remove and set aside to cool."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In the same pan, add the remaining 2 tablespoons of olive oil and the sliced onions. Cook over low heat for 15 minutes until very soft and translucent. Do not let them brown."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour in the white wine and white wine vinegar. Stir and let it bubble for 2 minutes. Add the raisins and pine nuts and stir to combine. Remove from the heat and leave to cool slightly."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "In a deep dish or container, layer half the fried sardines. Pour over half the onion mixture. Repeat with the remaining sardines and onion mixture, making sure the top layer is onion."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Cover and refrigerate for at least 24 hours before serving. Serve at room temperature with bread."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sardines falling apart when frying",
              "solution": "The fish was too wet or handled too much. Pat very dry and turn only once during frying. Use a wide spatula and work carefully."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Dish tastes too sharp after 24 hours",
              "solution": "Leave it for another day. The vinegar mellows significantly between 24 and 48 hours. A very small pinch of sugar stirred into the warm onion mixture can also soften the sharpness."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Onions turned brown",
              "solution": "The heat was too high. For saor, the onions must be cooked very slowly until soft but not coloured. Low heat for 15 minutes minimum."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Sarde in saor comes from Venice and the lagoon islands. Fishermen made it to preserve their catch on long trips. The sweet and sour flavour comes from Arab influence that reached Venice through its trade routes. Today it is a cichetto . a small bite served in the bars and bacari of Venice. It is one of the oldest dishes in the city."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
